WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:14.056
When a member reserves a book this library doesn't own, the request goes to the regional catalogue: a program, run by someone else, that knows what every library nearby holds.

2
00:00:14.056 --> 00:00:16.457
Its answer comes back as text fields.

3
00:00:16.457 --> 00:00:21.259
A status, and a few boxes that should be filled in only for some statuses.

4
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.610
A ready book has a pickup code and a hold shelf.

5
00:00:24.610 --> 00:00:26.705
An unavailable one has a reason.

6
00:00:26.705 --> 00:00:29.913
A place on the waiting list has an expected wait.

7
00:00:30.833 --> 00:00:33.619
Here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:33.619 --> 00:00:38.161
The comments say which fields go together, and everyone reads the comments.

9
00:00:38.161 --> 00:00:41.371
So what's the harm in one class with optional fields?

10
00:00:42.467 --> 00:00:46.860
Picture the answer as one paper form with every box printed on it.

11
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:51.653
Nothing stops someone from ticking Ready and also writing: reason, lost.

12
00:00:51.867 --> 00:00:54.630
So how many ways can this form be filled in?

13
00:00:54.630 --> 00:00:55.635
Pause and guess.

14
00:00:58.633 --> 00:00:59.825
Three statuses.

15
00:00:59.825 --> 00:01:07.294
Then four optional boxes, each filled or empty: two, times two, times two, times two, sixteen.

16
00:01:07.294 --> 00:01:09.360
Three times sixteen is 48.

17
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:11.267
And how many make sense?

18
00:01:11.267 --> 00:01:11.744
Three.

19
00:01:12.667 --> 00:01:20.145
In code, this form is a DTO, a data transfer object: a class that only holds a message's fields as they arrived.

20
00:01:20.145 --> 00:01:25.554
Only the comments say which ones go together, so every reader has to check again.

21
00:01:25.554 --> 00:01:27.023
And one of them won't.

22
00:01:27.233 --> 00:01:33.758
The lesson's demo hands a ready reply with no pickup code to the code that prints pickup slips.

23
00:01:33.758 --> 00:01:35.887
It prints: shelf 12, code null.

24
00:01:36.800 --> 00:01:42.330
So a member walks up with a slip that says: your book is on shelf 12, and your code is...

25
00:01:42.330 --> 00:01:42.827
nothing.

26
00:01:42.827 --> 00:01:46.120
The book is right there, and nobody can hand it over.

27
00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:48.854
The trouble isn't that someone forgot a box.

28
00:01:48.854 --> 00:01:50.593
It's that the form let them.

29
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:55.893
So let's print three forms instead, each with only its own boxes.

30
00:01:55.893 --> 00:01:57.852
Ready has a code and a shelf.

31
00:01:57.852 --> 00:01:59.542
Unavailable has a reason.

32
00:01:59.542 --> 00:02:01.704
Waitlisted has an expected wait.

33
00:02:01.704 --> 00:02:06.232
In code, that's a sealed interface, HoldResult, with three records.

34
00:02:06.433 --> 00:02:09.720
Now, ready with a reason can't even be written down.

35
00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:11.110
There's no box for it.

36
00:02:11.110 --> 00:02:13.575
Three shapes, and all three make sense.

37
00:02:13.575 --> 00:02:21.223
The checks that are left move into the constructors: a pickup code is six letters or digits, and hold shelves start at 1.

38
00:02:21.433 --> 00:02:24.293
Run the demo with the sealed model.

39
00:02:24.293 --> 00:02:28.542
The broken reply is refused: pickupCode is required.

40
00:02:28.542 --> 00:02:32.056
The good one prints: shelf 12, code K7P2QX.

41
00:02:32.967 --> 00:02:36.575
This has a name: make illegal states unrepresentable.

42
00:02:36.575 --> 00:02:42.565
Brian Goetz, Java's language architect, calls the whole style data-oriented programming.

43
00:02:42.565 --> 00:02:45.765
Records hold this and that: a code and a shelf.

44
00:02:45.765 --> 00:02:50.871
A sealed interface says this or that: ready, or unavailable, or waitlisted.

45
00:02:51.800 --> 00:02:57.334
But three clean forms only help if every answer from outside is copied onto one of them.

46
00:02:57.334 --> 00:03:00.290
Letters from outside arrive in any handwriting.

47
00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.302
So one clerk stands at the door.

48
00:03:02.302 --> 00:03:08.403
Each letter is copied onto the right form, or sent back with a polite note saying what was wrong.

49
00:03:09.333 --> 00:03:12.113
In code, that clerk is one function, parse.

50
00:03:12.113 --> 00:03:14.117
It switches on the status text.

51
00:03:14.117 --> 00:03:18.965
A ready reply needs its code and its shelf, or it's rejected with a reason.

52
00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:22.391
Anything it doesn't know, null included, is rejected.

53
00:03:22.391 --> 00:03:27.432
And when a constructor refuses a value, parse turns that into a rejection too.

54
00:03:27.432 --> 00:03:28.467
It never throws.

55
00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:32.906
Here's a sloppy reply: status READY, with a space at the end.

56
00:03:32.906 --> 00:03:34.422
What does the door do?

57
00:03:34.422 --> 00:03:35.525
Pause and guess.

58
00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:41.539
Rejected: unknown status, READY and a space.

59
00:03:41.539 --> 00:03:43.610
The pickup desk never sees it.

60
00:03:43.610 --> 00:03:49.549
A strict door turns a sloppy reply into a visible rejection, not a slip with bad data.

61
00:03:50.467 --> 00:03:52.877
Behind the door, nothing checks again.

62
00:03:52.877 --> 00:03:59.983
A method that takes a Ready knows it has a real code and a real shelf, because no other kind of Ready can exist.

63
00:03:59.983 --> 00:04:02.330
Check outside data once, at the door.

64
00:04:02.330 --> 00:04:04.424
Behind the door, trust the types.

65
00:04:05.333 --> 00:04:11.358
The rules that read these forms live in one binder at the desk: one function each, with one switch.

66
00:04:11.358 --> 00:04:13.244
A new rule is one new function.

67
00:04:13.244 --> 00:04:18.538
A new kind of form breaks every switch until it's handled, and the compiler lists them.

68
00:04:18.538 --> 00:04:22.555
The library's kinds of form rarely change, so that's a good trade.

69
00:04:23.467 --> 00:04:25.601
Here's where the picture breaks.

70
00:04:25.601 --> 00:04:27.935
A real door can't be walked around.

71
00:04:27.935 --> 00:04:28.535
Code can.

72
00:04:28.535 --> 00:04:36.070
Data that skips parse, or a nullable status field sneaked back into the records, brings the 48 shapes right back.

73
00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.604
The model is only as good as its edge.

74
00:04:39.533 --> 00:04:42.976
So what's the harm in one class with optional fields?

75
00:04:42.976 --> 00:04:45.445
It allows 48 shapes when 3 make sense.

76
00:04:45.445 --> 00:04:52.396
Give each valid shape its own record, check outside data once at the door, and keep the rules in functions.

77
00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:53.388
Quick check.

78
00:04:53.388 --> 00:04:58.248
A class has a status with two values, and three optional fields beside it.

79
00:04:58.248 --> 00:05:00.087
How many shapes can it take?

80
00:05:03.267 --> 00:05:06.054
Two, times two, times two, times two: 16.

81
00:05:06.054 --> 00:05:09.317
That's a lot of shapes for one comment to guard.
